No panic on anything a peer sends during or after the handshake

The UKEY2 handshake is unauthenticated: any device on the network can run
it, so everything it carries is hostile input. Upstream panics on some of
it, and each panic ends the connection task in the middle of whatever it
held (on the receiving side, possibly a half-written file):

- finalize_key_exchange builds the peer's P-256 point from the two
  coordinates it sent and `unwrap()`s PublicKey::from_encoded_point,
  which is None for any point that is not on the curve. One ClientFinish
  with made-up coordinates panics the receiver (and a ServerInit the
  sender) before anything is authenticated. The coordinates are also
  decoded wrongly: a longer one is cut to its last 32 bytes whatever the
  rest holds, and a shorter one (Java's BigInteger drops leading zero
  bytes, about one key in 128) is used as is and fails.
- Both handlers start with `encryption_done: true`, so a keep-alive or a
  disconnection sent before the key exchange (an application cancelling
  a transfer that is still handshaking) reaches `encrypt_key.unwrap()`.
- Keys, the commitment, the handshake messages and the own key's
  coordinates are all reached through `unwrap()`; the sequence counters
  overflow after 2^31 frames (a panic with overflow checks on).
- The sender panics on a local file name that is not UTF-8.

utils::decode_p256_point decodes a coordinate the way Java writes it
(sign byte, leading zeros dropped), refuses one that is longer, and
returns an error for a point not on the curve. Every `unwrap()` on the
handshake path is an error instead, both handlers start unencrypted, and
the sequence counters use checked arithmetic. The connection request's
endpoint info is parsed with bounds-checked reads, and the device type is
taken from the right bits (3 to 1 of the flags byte; upstream masked the
wrong ones). Unit tests cover the coordinate encodings, off-curve points
and garbage.
